A||N||3||brharris27370||4spd trans questions||||1||
Z||000000||brharris27370||12-05-2001||04:12 PM||||Are there any differences between the 4spd 4cyl trans and the 6cyl one.  Like is the bellhousing the same.  Shifting levers.  Axle splines that sort of thing.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||12-05-2001||05:44 PM||||Everything is the same except for the gear ratios.  The V-6 4-sp. has a 3.65 ratio, while the 4-cyl. 4-speeds have either a 3.32 or 4.10 ratio.<P>Dimensionally they are identical....all Fiero transmissions are interchangeable, so all have the same bellhousing bolt pattern, etc.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||jelly2m8||12-05-2001||06:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfiero:<BR><B>Everything is the same except for the gear ratios.  The V-6 4-sp. has a 3.65 ratio, while the 4-cyl. 4-speeds have either a 3.32 or 4.10 ratio.<P>Dimensionally they are identical....all Fiero transmissions are interchangeable, so all have the same bellhousing bolt pattern, etc.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>mrfiero is right on on this. Just swap the whole assembly. <BR> The only difference is internal, the 85-86 4 spds have bigger shift forks, and they won't fit inside the 84 casings. I have not tried putting 84 internals in a newer case, but I can't see this being a problem.<P> Just swap the transaxle assembly, and use which ever slave and slave mount that pertains to your year car.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||brharris27370||12-06-2001||08:14 AM||||Thanks for the info guys.  How hard is it to switch from 4spd to 5spd.<P>||||reg||1
